    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d383">

                <pb facs="0526" n="514" sameAs="frus:frus1943v03/pg514"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/28434: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the Soviet
                        Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Standley</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-03-18T21:00:00-04:00">March 18, 1943—9
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>152. Your 143, March 10, 4 p.m. We are grateful for the helpful analysis of
                    Soviet policies set forth in the first two sections<anchor n="45"
                        xml:id="d383-f1"/> of your telegram under reference and appreciate the
                    situation outlined in section S.<anchor n="46" xml:id="d383-f2"/> Nevertheless,
                    we are inclined to believe that it would not be opportune just now when Stalin
                    is undoubtedly preoccupied with the critical military situation to endeavor to
                    enter into a discussion with him of the character proposed.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
